<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: #ffffff; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0; font-size: 24px;">Lesson Planner – Theme # ___ – Lesson # 2</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 16px;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 20px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 2</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Reading</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 9th Grade (CEFR: A2.4)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Transportation</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> Rules of the Road</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Reading: Interpret written instructions for daily activities. Reading: Evaluate text structures and elements.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Reading: Can interpret written instructions on using the metro or metro bus in Panama City (e.g., "Explain steps for buying or recharging a Metrobus card at an Albrook station kiosk"). Can evaluate text structures in brochures about Panama's transit systems (e.g., "I can see sections like Routes, Ticket Prices, and Timetables. These headings help organize the information clearly."). Can interpret directions for navigating a local transit map (e.g., "To find the bus stop, follow the red line on the map. The bus stop is near the library, right after the park.").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 30px;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="background-color: #f2f2f2;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 10px; text-align: left; width: 33%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 10px; text-align: left; width: 33%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 10px; text-align: left; width: 34%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0 0 10px 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher greets students and asks them to form small groups. Each group receives a set of picture cards showing different transportation modes (bus, car, metro, bike, plane) and traffic signs (stop sign, pedestrian crossing, speed limit). Students match the signs to the correct mode of <span style="font-weight: bold;">transportation</span> they might encounter them with. They briefly discuss in English, "Which <span style="font-weight: bold;">vehicle</span> do you <span style="font-weight: bold;">prefer</span> for your daily <span style="font-weight: bold;">commute</span>?" and "What is one <span style="font-weight: bold;">rule</span> you <span style="font-weight: bold;">have to follow</span> when using it?"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher holds up a simplified visual of instructions for using a Metrobus card. The teacher says, "Today we will practice <span style="font-weight: bold;">reading</span> instructions like these. It's important to understand the <span style="font-weight: bold;">rules</span> for our <span style="font-weight: bold;">safety</span>. Look at this first step: '<span style="font-weight: bold;">Tap in</span> your card here.' I can see the word 'tap' (/æ/ not /eɪ/ like 'tape'). We say 'tap in' when you <span style="font-weight: bold;">board</span> the bus. You <span style="font-weight: bold;">get on</span> the bus. And you <span style="font-weight: bold;">get off</span> the bus." The teacher models pronunciation, emphasizing stress in "<span style="font-weight: bold;">transportation</span>" and "<span style="font-weight: bold;">passenger</span>."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Do you say 'get on' when you leave the bus?" (No, you say 'get off').</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is it important to understand <span style="font-weight: bold;">safety rules</span> when you <span style="font-weight: bold;">travel</span>?" (Yes, it is very important).</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0 0 10px 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Transportation")</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Students receive a printout of a short informational brochure titled "Your Guide to Safe Travel on Panama's Metro and Metrobus." The brochure includes sections like "Basic <span style="font-weight: bold;">Rules</span>," "Buying/Recharging your <span style="font-weight: bold;">Fare</span> Card," and "Important <span style="font-weight: bold;">Safety</span> Tips." It uses bullet points, short paragraphs, and clear headings. The text incorporates key vocabulary (e.g., <span style="font-weight: bold;">passenger</span>, <span style="font-weight: bold;">route</span>, <span style="font-weight: bold;">schedule</span>, <span style="font-weight: bold;">traffic light</span>, <span style="font-weight: bold;">seatbelt</span>, <span style="font-weight: bold;">delay</span>, <span style="font-weight: bold;">rush hour</span>) and grammar (e.g., "You <span style="font-weight: bold;">have to</span> keep your card charged," "You <span style="font-weight: bold;">should</span> hold onto handrails," "The Metro is <span style="font-weight: bold;">faster</span> than the bus during <span style="font-weight: bold;">rush hour</span>").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Reading comprehension for specific information and overall understanding of instructions and regulations related to public <span style="font-weight: bold;">transportation</span>.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist questions:</b> Students read the brochure quickly and answer: "What is the main purpose of this brochure?" (To inform about safe travel rules for Metro and Metrobus).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False:</b> Students read specific sentences from the text and decide if they are true or false (e.g., "You <span style="font-weight: bold;">should</span> always <span style="font-weight: bold;">wear</span> a <span style="font-weight: bold;">seatbelt</span> on the Metrobus." -> True).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information matching:</b> Students match headings from the brochure (e.g., "Basic <span style="font-weight: bold;">Rules</span>") to their descriptions (e.g., "Information about what <span style="font-weight: bold;">passengers</span> <span style="font-weight: bold;">have to follow</span>").</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0 0 10px 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Accuracy focus on Reading)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Reading to identify specific grammatical structures (modal verbs for obligation/advice, comparatives/superlatives) and target <span style="font-weight: bold;">vocabulary</span> related to <span style="font-weight: bold;">transportation</span> <span style="font-weight: bold;">rules</span> and <span style="font-weight: bold;">safety</span>.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice (Gap-fill):</b> Students receive a worksheet with sentences excerpted from the brochure but with blanks where key <span style="font-weight: bold;">vocabulary</span> (e.g., <span style="font-weight: bold;">fare</span>, <span style="font-weight: bold;">passenger</span>, <span style="font-weight: bold;">delay</span>, <span style="font-weight: bold;">seatbelt</span>) or modal verbs (e.g., <span style="font-weight: bold;">should</span>, <span style="font-weight: bold;">have to</span>) are missing. They fill in the blanks by re-reading the relevant sections of the brochure. Example: "You <span style="font-weight: bold;">____</span> (modal) <span style="font-weight: bold;">wear</span> your <span style="font-weight: bold;">____</span> (noun) on the Metrobus."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled (Sentence Transformation/Comparison):</b> Students work in pairs. They are given prompts to rephrase information from the brochure using comparatives/superlatives or phrasal verbs. Example: "The Metro is fast, but a <span style="font-weight: bold;">plane</span> is much <span style="font-weight: bold;">faster</span>." or "When you <span style="font-weight: bold;">board</span> the bus, you <span style="font-weight: bold;">get on</span> the bus." They compare different <span style="font-weight: bold;">transportation benefits</span> based on the text, e.g., "Which <span style="font-weight: bold;">transportation</span> is the <span style="font-weight: bold;">cheapest</span> for a long <span style="font-weight: bold;">journey</span>?" or "<span style="font-weight: bold;">Driving</span> is sometimes more <span style="font-weight: bold;">convenient</span> than public <span style="font-weight: bold;">transportation</span>, but public <span style="font-weight: bold;">transportation</span> is often more <span style="font-weight: bold;">efficient</span> in <span style="font-weight: bold;">traffic</span>." The teacher circulates, modeling correct pronunciation of words like "<span style="font-weight: bold;">convenience</span>" and clarifying vowel sounds in minimal pairs (e.g., /æ/ in "<span style="font-weight: bold;">tap</span>" vs /eɪ/ in "tape").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Interaction (Rule Scramble):</b> The teacher provides strips of paper with jumbled rules or instructions from the brochure. In pairs, students read the strips and put them in the correct logical order or match them to a specific section heading. They then discuss, "<span style="font-weight: bold;">Why do we have to follow this rule?</span>" or "<span style="font-weight: bold;">What are the <span style="font-weight: bold;">benefits</span> of following these <span style="font-weight: bold;">protocols</span>?</span>"</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15-18 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0 0 10px 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Transportation")</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task format:</b> "Transit Map Challenge." Students receive a simplified map of a short Metro or Metrobus <span style="font-weight: bold;">route</span> in Panama City with key landmarks (e.g., Albrook Station, Multiplaza, Paitilla). Alongside the map, they receive short, written directions for a specific <span style="font-weight: bold;">journey</span> or a set of safety notices for different sections of the <span style="font-weight: bold;">route</span> (e.g., "Caution: <span style="font-weight: bold;">Crowded</span> during <span style="font-weight: bold;">rush hour</span> at Albrook. You <span style="font-weight: bold;">should avoid</span> this if possible." or "To <span style="font-weight: bold;">catch</span> the Metrobus to Multiplaza, <span style="font-weight: bold;">hop on</span> at Balboa Avenue. The <span style="font-weight: bold;">fare</span> is $0.25.").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connect directly to Theme:</b> "Rules of the Road" and <span style="font-weight: bold;">Scenario: "Transportation"</span>. Students <span style="font-weight: bold;">read</span> the map and accompanying text. In pairs, they then "plan" a <span style="font-weight: bold;">journey</span> from Point A to Point B, orally explaining the steps using instructions from the text and incorporating <span style="font-weight: bold;">safety</span> considerations. For example, "To get to Multiplaza, you <span style="font-weight: bold;">should get on</span> the bus at Balboa. It is the <span style="font-weight: bold;">safest</span> <span style="font-weight: bold;">route</span>." or "The Metro is often <span style="font-weight: bold;">faster</span> and more <span style="font-weight: bold;">efficient</span>, but you <span style="font-weight: bold;">have to</span> transfer." They focus on making suggestions for <span style="font-weight: bold;">convenience</span> and emphasizing <span style="font-weight: bold;">safety</span>.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Focus:</b> Fluency in interpreting written and visual information, collaborative interaction, and communicative task completion by explaining the <span style="font-weight: bold;">journey</span> clearly.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">8-10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0 0 10px 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Formative assessment strategy)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Individual students write 2-3 sentences summarizing the most important <span style="font-weight: bold;">safety rules</span> for using public <span style="font-weight: bold;">transportation</span> based on the brochure and map. Alternatively, they answer a question like, "Explain the steps for buying or recharging a Metrobus card at an Albrook station kiosk, based on the text you read."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clear interpretation of written instructions and information.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Correct use of at least two target <span style="font-weight: bold;">vocabulary</span> words (e.g., <span style="font-weight: bold;">passenger</span>, <span style="font-weight: bold;">fare</span>, <span style="font-weight: bold;">seatbelt</span>).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Appropriate use of modal verbs (e.g., <span style="font-weight: bold;">should</span>, <span style="font-weight: bold;">have to</span>).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Ability to evaluate text structures (e.g., referring to specific sections or headings).</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete an exit ticket with the following prompts:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I can now understand instructions about using public <span style="font-weight: bold;">transportation</span> in English. (Yes/Partially/No)"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"One new <span style="font-weight: bold;">rule</span> or word I learned today is: <span style="font-weight: bold;">____</span>."</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0 0 10px 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What part of the reading activities helped me the most to understand the <span style="font-weight: bold;">rules</span> and instructions?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How can understanding these <span style="font-weight: bold;">transportation rules</span> help me in real life in Panama?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> The brochure and map activities effectively engaged students in <span style="font-weight: bold;">reading</span> for both gist and specific details. The focus on modal verbs and <span style="font-weight: bold;">comparatives/superlatives</span> within the context of <span style="font-weight: bold;">transportation benefits</span> and <span style="font-weight: bold;">safety</span> was well-received. Some students needed more time to analyze the map. For the next lesson (Speaking & Interaction), I will ensure more scaffolded opportunities for students to orally summarize and discuss the <span style="font-weight: bold;">rules</span> and <span style="font-weight: bold;">benefits</span>, perhaps starting with simpler dialogues or role-play scenarios related to asking for and giving directions or advice on <span style="font-weight: bold;">travel</span>.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> The next lesson will transition from <span style="font-weight: bold;">reading</span> about <span style="font-weight: bold;">transportation rules</span> to actively speaking and interacting about <span style="font-weight: bold;">transportation</span> options and <span style="font-weight: bold;">safety protocols</span>, using the vocabulary and grammar practiced today.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>